      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#1 · the P1 encounter, lines 6 and 10; the funhouse capture (human-amplified: yes)",
          "title": "The figure caught in the glass",
          "italic": "The capture demonstrates more than misrepresentation: the distortion can absorb the person's means of recognising misrepresentation — a displeasing reflection holds as firmly as a flattering one.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "In the funhouse scene the glass alters the figure that seeks a likeness. Its distortions are fascinating: beauty and ghoulishness both hold the spectator. Taylor invokes Narcissus for this captivation while giving the technological image its own agency. The spectator's attachment is to the image that seems to disclose who the spectator is; as attention contracts around that disclosure, the capacity to feel and voice its distortion weakens — Taylor's severe image of humanity reduced to organs of sight makes the loss",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "bodily: the gaze remains occupied while living comparison falls away. The distortion does more than misrepresent: it can absorb the means of recognising misrepresentation. What is at stake is the relation between the image and the life seeking itself there; the return must reopen that relation to release the captivated figure. A20 qualifies the transfer; the later motion must return a living capacity for judgment to the people whose judgment entered the glass. No additional ancient episode is supplied for Narcissus.\n\nOccurrences: the funhouse distortions (#1); Narcissus's absorption (#1); the organs-of-sight loss (#1, answered at #3's initiated return).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
