      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#2 · the causal distinctions; Bratton's Agentworld brief compared (human-amplified: yes for the ratified comparison)",
          "title": "The image becomes a measure",
          "italic": "The reversal demonstrates the danger of the active idol: the mirror's earlier dependence disappears behind its later efficacy — and the direction in which reciprocal influence is used remains decisive.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The glass now leads a movement. A model predicts, ranks, classifies, recommends or addresses; people subsequently encounter a field partly arranged by that intervention. A ranking directs attention; a forecast enters a decision that changes the forecast's object; a generated persona offers a manner of speaking its user begins to inhabit. The next reflection receives more than the world from which the first was made: it receives consequences of the mirror's own earlier activity. The exteriorised measure has become one of humanity's measures of itself. If the model's categories acquire exclusive authority, resistance is processed through the image's own",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "terms: the person appears as a poor fit, an anomalous case, a failure of the selected gauge — the mirror's earlier dependence disappearing behind its later efficacy, the danger of the active idol. The causal distinctions matter: feedback returns a consequence as input; performativity makes a representation operative in a practice; a self-fulfilling prediction requires conduct helping produce what was predicted; a forecast can change the field and still be false. Bratton's brief compares the reversal in which agents model humans and become models for human self-modelling; its scenarios sharpen the encounter without supplying Taylor's whole or its vocation.\n\nOccurrences: the ranking, forecast and persona (#2); the anomaly-processing of resistance (#2, #3); the Bratton comparison (#2, bounded at #5→0).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
